{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS CloudTrail launches Insights for data events to automatically detect anomalies in data access - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail Insights, a feature that helps identify and respond to unusual activity in AWS accounts, has been expanded to include data events. Previously, Insights analyzed only management events, but now it also monitors data events, enhancing the ability to detect potential security or operational issues. Insights for data events automatically detects anomalies in data access activities, such as increased delete Amazon S3 object API calls or error rates for AWS Lambda function invocations. This feature establishes normal baselines for data access patterns and alerts users when it detects anomalies, providing the relevant data events from the anomaly period for precise investigation. Users can configure alerts for potential issues and respond rapidly to threats or issues. Insights for data events is available in all regions where AWS CloudTrail is available, and additional charges apply for this feature. To get started, users should refer to the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/cloudtrail-insights-data-events-detect-anomalies-access/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS CloudTrail Enables Option to Add All Amazon S3 Buckets to Data Events","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service that records API calls for usage analysis, now enables automatic addition of Amazon S3 buckets to S3 Data Events. Previously, users had to manually add each S3 bucket to track object-level operations, but now, with a few clicks, all new and existing S3 buckets can be logged. This feature is available in multiple regions including US, Canada, Asia Pacific, EU, and South America","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2017/09/aws-cloudtrail-enables-option-to-add-all-amazon-s3-buckets-to-data-events/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"querying-aws-s3","skill_description":"Queries S3 object metadata, tracks bucket activity, audits object changes, searches annotations, and analyzes storage metrics using S3 Metadata system tables (journal, inventory, annotation) and S3 Storage Lens tables via Athena SQL. Applies when counting objects, finding recent uploads or deletions, identifying who wrote to a prefix, breaking down storage classes, finding objects by tag, searching annotation content, analyzing storage lens metrics, or enabling S3 Metadata tracking. Prefers system tables over raw S3 APIs (list-objects-v2, head-object) at scale. Trigger phrases: bucket activity, object count, who uploaded, track deletions, storage class breakdown, find by tag, search annotations, storage lens metrics, audit bucket changes.\n\nServices: s3, s3tables, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-s3"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-investigation","skill_description":"Investigates what is happening in an AWS account from evidence already recorded there. Not for capturing new data from running code (snapshots of function arguments on a live service, Dynamic Instrumentation), questions answered from the CloudWatch Omni context graph or Application Signals service map, or building or changing infrastructure. Covers reading deployment records instead of the newest stored version (completed AppConfig deployments, the version an alias points to, a stack's current template), following a request through every service's own logs (exact per-layer fields, unique failed requests in an alarm's breach window, failure boundary per event), and answering questions from the account's data (exact counts and breakdowns, coverage of the requested range, exact filters and joins, the rule behind failed or unmatched records, verbatim values). Use to triage an alarm, error spike or failed request, trace a request across services, or count, break down, extract or join records.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, appconfig, lambda, cloudformation, dynamodb, s3, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, investigate, query, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability, operations","skill_name":"aws-investigation"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS docs on CloudTrail data events for S3 and Lambda", "parent_id": "d29e8cbb-9fb5-476a-b0f6-29b67101d2a6"}

